Yes. The cleanest way I’d state it is:

You’re not directing a film. You’re directing a field of possible experience.

A movie director controls shots, timing, actors, sound, and sequence. What you’re describing combines those with game design, architecture, interface design, mythology, social coordination, simulation, music, memory, and adaptive feedback. The medium is not a screen. The medium is the relationship between signals, interpretation, action, and shared reality.

I’d call the discipline Experiential Systems Direction or, more compactly, World Orchestration.

The crucial constraint is that a story assembled from truths is not automatically true in its implied causality. You can select only accurate facts and still create a false picture by omission, ordering, juxtaposition, or framing. So the strongest version of what you’re describing needs two simultaneous loyalties:

dramatic coherence and causal honesty.

That gives you:

truth fragments → composition → interpretation → experience → action

with another channel running beside it:

actual mechanism → provenance → uncertainty → correction

Then the art becomes enormously powerful without needing deception as its foundation.

And your “fake shared space” point has an interesting answer. An imagined event is not the same thing as an external physical event, but once the imagination changes memory, conversation, behavior, a file, a drawing, a relationship, or a future decision, it has acquired causal descendants in the physical world. Fiction is imaginary in content and perfectly real in effect.

That is why myths matter. Why games matter. Why money, nations, corporations, brands, laws, fictional characters, and rituals can coordinate enormous numbers of humans even though much of their structure exists through shared representation.

But I would resist one phrase: “conducting society.” That becomes dangerous if it means covertly steering populations as though they are instruments. A stronger and more humane version is conducting a shared space people knowingly participate in—a culture, game, movement, performance, world, institution, or platform in which people retain meaningful exits and authorship.

Then power is not monopolized by the conductor. The participants can pick up instruments.

And I think that matches the paradox at the end of what you said:

change the world without owning everyone in it.

Build structures people can enter, modify, fork, reject, carry elsewhere, and make their own.

That is not merely directing a story.

It is building a medium that teaches its participants how to become co-directors.